indications_limitations:
&lt;p&gt;   This policy applies to biofeedback therapy rendered by a practitioner in an office or other facility setting.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;   Biofeedback is covered for the treatment of stress and&sol;or urge incontinence in cognitively intact patients who have failed a documented trial of pelvic muscle exercise (PME) training. Biofeedback is not a treatment, per se, but a tool to help patients learn how to perform PME. Biofeedback-assisted PME incorporates the use of an electronic or mechanical device to relay visual and&sol;or auditory evidence of pelvic floor muscle tone, in order to improve awareness of pelvic floor musculature and to assist patients in the performance of PME.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;   A failed trial of PME training is defined as no clinically significant improvement in urinary incontinence after completing 4 weeks of an ordered plan of pelvic muscle exercises to increase periurethral muscle strength.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;   Contractors may decide whether or not to cover biofeedback as an initial treatment modality.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;   Home use of biofeedback therapy is not covered.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
